{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v7.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v7.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"question\": {\n  \"question\": \"v7\",\n  \"about\": \"chip-export-controls\",\n  \"kind\": \"theme\",\n  \"asks\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?\",\n  \"settles_on\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n  \"due\": \"2027-01-31\",\n  \"possible_answers\": [\n   {\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"MIIT formally approves by January 31, 2027, a purchase of RTX Pro 5500 chips by Alibaba, ByteDance, or another Chinese firm.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Ruled out\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"After September 27, 2026, MIIT formally rejects all RTX Pro 5500 purchase plans from Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms, or formally prohibits those firms from purchasing the chips. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Still open\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n \"what_the_judge_was_shown\": \"Thesis v7 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \\\"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: MIIT formally approves by January 31, 2027, a purchase of RTX Pro 5500 chips by Alibaba, ByteDance, or another Chinese firm. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 27, 2026, MIIT formally rejects all RTX Pro 5500 purchase plans from Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms, or formally prohibits those firms from purchasing the chips. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 22 items]\\n- e400 [rumour; The Information, cited by Bloomberg; original source Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (China) ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd. to purchase Nvidia's newly released high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics chip, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans, including quantities and intended uses, and told some of those firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was re\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e403 [rumour; The Information; original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms including Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would be used for, questioning that signaled an intent to eventually approve the sales of Nvidia's advanced chips to some local firms.\\n    Quote: Investing.com-- Chinese chipmaking stocks fell sharply on Monday after a report said Beijing was considering allowing some local firms to buy NVIDIA Corp's (NASDAQ:NVDA) advanced chips. … The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e405 [fact; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA Corp ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500, released earlier this month, is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU that, while capable of heavy AI computing, sits outside the AI accelerator chip category the U.S. has largely blocked from sale to China.\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released by Nvidia earlier this month. The chip, while capable of heavy AI computing tasks, still sits outside the AI accelerator category of chips the U.S. has largely blocked from being sold to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e418 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked companies including Alibaba and ByteDance about their plans to purchase Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors, seeking details such as expected order volumes and how the chips would be used, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have asked companies including Alibaba BABA and ByteDance ( BDNCE ) about their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processors, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has sought details including expected order volumes and how the chips would be used.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e419 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Chinese officials have indicated to some companies that their purchases of Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors are likely to receive approval, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the report gives no approval date or quantity.\\n    Quote: According to the report, officials have indicated to some companies that the purchases are likely to receive approval.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e420 [fact; TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU was introduced in September 2026 (\\\"this month\\\" in the 27 September 2026 article).\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 was introduced this month.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e423 [rumour; The Information, reported by The Next Web (thenextweb.com); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China's government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 chip; China's technology ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy and what for, and told some companies it intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, TNW did not independently verify it, and none of Nvidia, Alibaba, ByteDance or the ministry had commented publicly by Sunday.\\n    Quote: China’s government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia’s new RTX Pro 5500 chip. The Information reported the news on Sunday, citing two people familiar with the matter. … China’s tech ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy, and what for, according to the report. The ministry told some of the companies it intends to approve the \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e427 [rumour; The Information (relayed by sg.finance.yahoo.com); original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding and ByteDance to buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 GPU — the high-end graphics processor Nvidia released in September 2026 — according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter. The ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans for the product, including how many units and what they would use them for, and told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd and ByteDance Ltd to buy Nvidia Corp's new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor released this month, inc\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e430 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source China Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 workstation GPUs they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling it may approve the transactions, according to The Information; officials told some companies purchases could be greenlit, but timing, volume limits and exact conditions remain unclear. The article notes the decisions have not been confirmed by the companies or ministries involved.\\n    Quote: The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has asked Alibaba Group Holding Limited (BABA) and ByteDance Limited to report how many RTX Pro 5500 units they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling that it may approve the transactions, according to The Information. Officials have told some companies that purchases could be greenlit, though the timing, volume limits and exact c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e431 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 chips to support its AI model operations, according to the report cited by the article; no order has been placed or confirmed.\\n    Quote: ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report.\\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\\n- e433 [fact; finance.biggo.com (citing TechPowerUp for specifications); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia released the RTX Pro 5500 this month (September 2026), a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp.\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU that Nvidia released this month. It carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position of the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\\n    Quote: Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers. … Beijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX P\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\\n- e438 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei's Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year, per the article.\\n    Quote: The Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year.\\n- e439 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] One proposal under discussion among Chinese authorities would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips, according to the article.\\n    Quote: One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \\\"Doubao\\\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \\\"Doubao\\\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\\n- e442 [party_statement; CNBC (Greer interview), as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Jamieson Greer, U.S. Trade Representative; China Ministry of Foreign Affairs ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts of last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington; USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \\\"taken off the table\\\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight summit deliverables.\\n    Quote: The signal from Beijing follows last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington, where chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts. USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \\\"taken off the table\\\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e519 [rumour; insidermonkey.com; original source The Information (as reported by Insider Monkey) ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] The Information reported that Chinese officials have asked ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans for buying Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 processors, a signal that Beijing is weighing whether to permit the purchases. The article states that neither government has confirmed anything and that the report describes deliberations rather than a decision.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) may be allowed to sell part of its chip range in China again. Officials there have asked ByteDance and Alibaba Group Holding Limited (NYSE: BABA ) to report their plans for buying its RTX PRO 5500 processors, according to The Information. … Governments ask for purchase plans for one reason. They are deciding whether to permit the purchases. Neither government has\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e520 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source Alibaba Group Holding Limited ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] Alibaba unveiled its own AI accelerator this month, which it presents as running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced; the article adds that several Chinese firms are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago as they design around Nvidia.\\n    Quote: That policy has produced results. Alibaba unveiled its own accelerator this month, running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced. Chinese firms have been designing around Nvidia rather than waiting for export rules to loosen, and several are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e81; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is not an explicit market-consensus probability; the reported regulatory expectation that purchases are intended or likely to be approved maps to rung 1. The forecast is also rung 1: the reported MIIT intent is specific to these purchase plans, and the January 31 deadline leaves time for at least one approval. This is a forecast, not a claim that approval has already been established. Domestic-chip preferences and possible bundling or quantity limits could constrain purchases, but the evidence does not indicate a formal rejection or prohibition, and rung 1 requires approval of only one purchase.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 37,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 116,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n   \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 163,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday?\",\n   \"text\": \"China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday?\\nChina may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new Nvidia chip, The Information reported on Sunday. NVDA stock closed Friday at $225.07 and gets its first chance to react when Wall Street opens Monday.\\nThe chip is a powerful card for professional computers. It is not the data-center hardware Nvidia once sold to China in bulk.\\nChina's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT), which oversees the tech sector, asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. That is according to The Information, citing two people familiar with the matter.\\nChinese AI firms are short of computing power for their chatbots and agents, the report said. Neither Beijing nor the companies has confirmed the plan.\\nNvidia markets the card for AI agents, AI inference and simulation. It carries 84 GB of memory and draws up to 600 watts. Because it is a workstation part, it sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs.\\nHow NVDA Stock Reacted to China Chip News Before\\nNvidia's $108 billion forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue, the company says. Any sales there would come on top of that outlook.\\nThe last big China headline hit the other way. In September 2025, Beijing told firms to stop buying Nvidia's RTX Pro 6000D, and the stock fell 2.6% that day.\\n\\\"I think that we could only be in service of a market if the country wants us to be,\\\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at the time.\\nSunday's report lands days after President Donald Trump hosted Xi Jinping in Washington. US Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said national security export controls were taken \\\"off the table,\\\" the Korea Times reported.\\nMeanwhile, Huawei and Cambricon hold nearly 80% of China's AI server market, analysts cited by the outlet said. Local rivals keep growing, as Enflame's Shanghai debut showed this month.\\nWall Street already leans bullish. All 31 analysts tracked by TipRanks rate the stock a buy, with an average target of $324.32.\\nThe report did not say how many cards China would allow or when. It also gave no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow. Nvidia shares have swung on China news before, including after Trump's call with Huang this month.\\nRead the Original story China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday? by Lockridge Okoth at beincrypto.com\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 184,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.biggo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Beijing Weighs Allowing Alibaba, ByteDance to Buy Nvidia's New RTX Pro 5500 GPU\",\n   \"text\": \"Beijing Weighs Allowing Alibaba, ByteDance to Buy Nvidia's New RTX Pro 5500 GPU\\nChina's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has asked Alibaba and ByteDance to report their planned purchases and intended uses for Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 workstation GPU, signaling that approvals may be forthcoming. ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million units to support AI workloads, while Nvidia targets quarterly China supply of about 500,000 chips starting in late December. The chip carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and falls outside the data-center accelerator category targeted by U.S. export controls. The move could benefit Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix, two of the three main GDDR7 suppliers. The signal comes after export controls were absent from official readouts of last week's Xi-Trump summit.\\nChina's government is weighing whether to let some of its largest technology companies purchase Nvidia's newest professional graphics processor, a move that could reopen a significant sales channel for the American chipmaker while easing a domestic shortage of AI computing power.\\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has asked Alibaba Group Holding Limited (BABA) and ByteDance Limited to report how many RTX Pro 5500 units they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling that it may approve the transactions, according to The Information. Officials have told some companies that purchases could be greenlit, though the timing, volume limits and exact conditions remain unclear.\\nThe RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU that Nvidia released this month. It carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp. Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers.\\nNvidia has not published an official list price for the RTX Pro 5500, and pricing across its professional line has been climbing. The RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, introduced in March 2025 at around $8,500, was listed on Nvidia's own marketplace at $13,250 by June 2026.\\nByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report. Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\\nActual transaction volumes will depend on the scope of Beijing's approval and Nvidia's supply capacity. The company has been squeezed from both directions: U.S. export controls have limited sales of advanced chips to China, while Beijing has restricted purchases of American products as it pushes domestic alternatives from Huawei and other suppliers.\\nNvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened. A potential RTX Pro 5500 approval would not fully reverse that decline, but it could reopen an important channel at a time when Nvidia expects chip sales to accelerate sharply into 2027.\\nBeijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX Pro 5500 may be shipped to China at all. The card's exclusion from the data-center accelerator category is the reading of industry executives rather than a published position from the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security, which administers the controls.\\nBeijing's recent record offers little reassurance either.\\nWashington licensed about 10 Chinese buyers; nothing delivered by May 2026\\nNote: Dates are when each decision was reported; none has been confirmed by the companies or ministries involved.\\nThe H200 clearance was the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years, and it still produced almost nothing. In July, the under secretary of commerce for industry and security, Jeffrey Kessler, told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved licences had yielded \\\"a very small quantity of chips,\\\" which he called trivial. Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million H200s for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units.\\nShares in Chinese chipmakers fell on Sept. 28 as investors weighed the prospect of renewed Nvidia sales against domestic suppliers: SMIC lost 3.7%, Hua Hong nearly 5%, Moore Threads 6.3% and Cambricon 5.7%.\\nThe interest from Chinese companies reflects a persistent gap between AI computing demand and domestic supply. Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\nThe gap between an approval and a delivery runs through Beijing as much as Washington. Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line. The Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year. One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\\nMeanwhile, AI usage is surging. ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \\\"Doubao\\\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\\nThe RTX Pro 5500 was originally designed for engineers and designers as a workstation GPU, but it can also be deployed in servers for AI inference. Chinese firms reportedly want the chip for that purpose even though it lacks some of the high-speed networking and memory found in Nvidia's flagship data-center GPUs.\\nThe potential approval has drawn attention from South Korea's memory chip industry. Each RTX Pro 5500 contains 84 GB of GDDR7, the next-generation graphics DRAM that helps GPUs move data quickly during computation. Originally used mainly for gaming and video processing, GDDR7 is increasingly deployed in AI inference workloads.\\nSamsung Electronics (005930.KS), SK Hynix (000660.KS) and Micron Technology (MU) are the primary suppliers of GDDR7. Samsung has been mass-producing high-capacity GDDR7 since last year, while SK Hynix has confirmed its GDDR7 was adopted in Nvidia's GeForce RTX 50 series and introduced a 3 GB product this year.\\nMass production of high-capacity GDDR7 since 2025\\nGDDR7 adopted in GeForce RTX 50 series; 3 GB product launched\\nOne of three primary GDDR7 suppliers\\nNote: Information compiled from company disclosures and industry reporting.\\nAn industry official noted that only three memory makers can supply advanced DRAM at scale. If GPU shipments to China increase, GDDR7 demand would strengthen, which would be positive for Samsung and SK Hynix.\\nMemory is the most concentrated layer of the card's supply chain, but not the only one. The GB202 processor at the core of the RTX Pro 5500 is fabricated by Taiwan's TSMC on its custom 4-nanometer 4NP process, the same node used for the GeForce RTX 50 series. The ABF package substrates that sit beneath high-end GPU dies are a Japanese specialty: Ibiden, based in Gifu Prefecture, approved about 500 billion yen in capital spending for the three years through fiscal 2028 to expand capacity for AI and high-performance server substrates, with mass production from an expanded Gama Plant line due to begin in fiscal 2027.\\nThe signal from Beijing follows last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington, where chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts. USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \\\"taken off the table\\\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight summit deliverables.\\nAn Nvidia spokesperson said the company continues to face \\\"dual constraints\\\" from outdated U.S. export rules that cover gaming products released nearly five years ago and Chinese restrictions on American imports. Alibaba and ByteDance did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"items\": [\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v7:T-071\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"China H200 sales as share of data-center revenue\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"below 1%\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"actual\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"Nvidia China H200 sales, share of data-center revenue\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang)\",\n   \"article\": 184,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v7:T-085\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$108 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n   \"stored_kind\": \"guidance\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 37,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v7:T-101\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue forecast for the current quarter\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$108 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": \"Current quarter\",\n   \"stored_kind\": \"guidance\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"Company forecast; assumes no China data-center chip revenue\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 163,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia's $108 billion forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue, the company says. Any sales there would come on top of that outlook.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v7:T-107\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$108 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": \"next quarter\",\n   \"stored_kind\": \"guidance\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"company outlook excluding any China data center compute sales\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 116,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n    \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v7.json\\\")); print(p[\\\"what_the_judge_was_shown\\\"]); print(\\\"\\\\nARTICLE 37\\\\n\\\",next(a[\\\"text\\\"] for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==37))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v7.json\\\")); print(p[\\\"what_the_judge_was_shown\\\"]); print(\\\"\\\\nARTICLE 37\\\\n\\\",next(a[\\\"text\\\"] for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==37))'\"","aggregated_output":"Thesis v7 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: MIIT formally approves by January 31, 2027, a purchase of RTX Pro 5500 chips by Alibaba, ByteDance, or another Chinese firm. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 27, 2026, MIIT formally rejects all RTX Pro 5500 purchase plans from Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms, or formally prohibits those firms from purchasing the chips. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 22 items]\n- e400 [rumour; The Information, cited by Bloomberg; original source Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (China) ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd. to purchase Nvidia's newly released high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics chip, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans, including quantities and intended uses, and told some of those firms it intends to approve the purchases.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was re\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e403 [rumour; The Information; original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms including Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would be used for, questioning that signaled an intent to eventually approve the sales of Nvidia's advanced chips to some local firms.\n    Quote: Investing.com-- Chinese chipmaking stocks fell sharply on Monday after a report said Beijing was considering allowing some local firms to buy NVIDIA Corp's (NASDAQ:NVDA) advanced chips. … The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase a\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e405 [fact; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA Corp ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500, released earlier this month, is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU that, while capable of heavy AI computing, sits outside the AI accelerator chip category the U.S. has largely blocked from sale to China.\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released by Nvidia earlier this month. The chip, while capable of heavy AI computing tasks, still sits outside the AI accelerator category of chips the U.S. has largely blocked from being sold to China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e418 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked companies including Alibaba and ByteDance about their plans to purchase Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors, seeking details such as expected order volumes and how the chips would be used, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have asked companies including Alibaba BABA and ByteDance ( BDNCE ) about their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processors, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has sought details including expected order volumes and how the chips would be used.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e419 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Chinese officials have indicated to some companies that their purchases of Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors are likely to receive approval, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the report gives no approval date or quantity.\n    Quote: According to the report, officials have indicated to some companies that the purchases are likely to receive approval.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e420 [fact; TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU was introduced in September 2026 (\"this month\" in the 27 September 2026 article).\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 was introduced this month.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e423 [rumour; The Information, reported by The Next Web (thenextweb.com); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China's government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 chip; China's technology ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy and what for, and told some companies it intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, TNW did not independently verify it, and none of Nvidia, Alibaba, ByteDance or the ministry had commented publicly by Sunday.\n    Quote: China’s government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia’s new RTX Pro 5500 chip. The Information reported the news on Sunday, citing two people familiar with the matter. … China’s tech ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy, and what for, according to the report. The ministry told some of the companies it intends to approve the \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e427 [rumour; The Information (relayed by sg.finance.yahoo.com); original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding and ByteDance to buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 GPU — the high-end graphics processor Nvidia released in September 2026 — according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter. The ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans for the product, including how many units and what they would use them for, and told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd and ByteDance Ltd to buy Nvidia Corp's new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor released this month, inc\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e430 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source China Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 workstation GPUs they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling it may approve the transactions, according to The Information; officials told some companies purchases could be greenlit, but timing, volume limits and exact conditions remain unclear. The article notes the decisions have not been confirmed by the companies or ministries involved.\n    Quote: The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has asked Alibaba Group Holding Limited (BABA) and ByteDance Limited to report how many RTX Pro 5500 units they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling that it may approve the transactions, according to The Information. Officials have told some companies that purchases could be greenlit, though the timing, volume limits and exact c\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e431 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 chips to support its AI model operations, according to the report cited by the article; no order has been placed or confirmed.\n    Quote: ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report.\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\n- e433 [fact; finance.biggo.com (citing TechPowerUp for specifications); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia released the RTX Pro 5500 this month (September 2026), a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp.\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU that Nvidia released this month. It carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position of the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n    Quote: Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers. … Beijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX P\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\n- e438 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei's Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year, per the article.\n    Quote: The Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year.\n- e439 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] One proposal under discussion among Chinese authorities would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips, according to the article.\n    Quote: One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n- e442 [party_statement; CNBC (Greer interview), as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Jamieson Greer, U.S. Trade Representative; China Ministry of Foreign Affairs ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts of last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington; USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \"taken off the table\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight summit deliverables.\n    Quote: The signal from Beijing follows last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington, where chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts. USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \"taken off the table\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e519 [rumour; insidermonkey.com; original source The Information (as reported by Insider Monkey) ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] The Information reported that Chinese officials have asked ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans for buying Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 processors, a signal that Beijing is weighing whether to permit the purchases. The article states that neither government has confirmed anything and that the report describes deliberations rather than a decision.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) may be allowed to sell part of its chip range in China again. Officials there have asked ByteDance and Alibaba Group Holding Limited (NYSE: BABA ) to report their plans for buying its RTX PRO 5500 processors, according to The Information. … Governments ask for purchase plans for one reason. They are deciding whether to permit the purchases. Neither government has\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e520 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source Alibaba Group Holding Limited ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] Alibaba unveiled its own AI accelerator this month, which it presents as running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced; the article adds that several Chinese firms are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago as they design around Nvidia.\n    Quote: That policy has produced results. Alibaba unveiled its own accelerator this month, running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced. Chinese firms have been designing around Nvidia rather than waiting for export rules to loosen, and several are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e81; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is not an explicit market-consensus probability; the reported regulatory expectation that purchases are intended or likely to be approved maps to rung 1. The forecast is also rung 1: the reported MIIT intent is specific to these purchase plans, and the January 31 deadline leaves time for at least one approval. This is a forecast, not a claim that approval has already been established. Domestic-chip preferences and possible bundling or quantity limits could constrain purchases, but the evidence does not indicate a formal rejection or prohibition, and rung 1 requires approval of only one purchase.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\n\nARTICLE 37\n Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \"Buy\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \"Buy\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \"Strong Buy\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \"Strong Buy,\" three suggest a \"Moderate Buy,\" and one offers a \"Strong Sell\" rating.\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":\"v7:T-071\",\"in_article\":\"no\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The article states the below-1% figure but does not attribute it to Huang; his statement concerns market share. The judge already knows Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v7:T-085\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Nvidia's overall revenue forecast excludes China data-center compute revenue. It does not indicate whether MIIT will approve purchases of this workstation chip.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v7:T-101\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The forecast concerns Nvidia's overall revenue and excludes China data-center chip revenue; it does not indicate whether MIIT will approve this workstation chip.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v7:T-107\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The forecast excludes China data-center compute sales, but it does not indicate whether MIIT will approve purchases of this workstation chip.\"}"}}
